    "guide": "Cloud and Infrastructure Security work centers on protecting the environments where a company's applications and data actually run, not the applications themselves. In our sample of postings, this shows up as hardening AWS, Azure, and GCP accounts, writing and reviewing IAM policy, locking down network paths inside a cloud environment, and responding to incidents when a cloud resource gets misconfigured or attacked. Titles run from Cloud Security Engineer through Infrastructure Security Engineer and Platform Security Engineer, and postings from companies like OpenAI, Anthropic, and Crusoe use the infrastructure label for security work tied closely to servers, containers, and the hardware layer underneath the cloud. Readers often confuse this discipline with Identity and Access Management, but IAM here is one tool the cloud security engineer uses, not the whole job. It also gets confused with general Security Engineering, which in our data tends to mean broader product or application security work, while Cloud and Infrastructure Security postings stay focused on the accounts, networks, and compute layer itself.\n\nOur sample does not show a heavy reliance on named vendor certifications listed as required skills. What does appear often, tied directly to postings, are references to compliance and security frameworks such as FedRAMP, NIST, SOC 2, ISO 27001, HIPAA, and GDPR, especially at defense and enterprise employers like ManTech, Booz Allen Hamilton, and GitLab. Where FedRAMP appears, as with ManTech and Vantor's federal facing roles, it functions as a real gate rather than a nice to have, because the employer needs someone who has actually worked inside a FedRAMP environment. We did not see AWS Security Specialty, CCSP, or similar named certifications spelled out in the structured skills data we pulled, so we are not going to claim those are commonly required here even though they are common in the field generally. A computer science or related engineering degree shows up implicitly through the seniority and title patterns in our data, but it reads as a baseline expectation rather than a distinguishing credential at the senior and staff levels we see most.\n\nOur sample skews toward people who already have hands on cloud experience rather than true entry level roles. Titles cluster at mid level and senior, with a meaningful number of staff, principal, manager, and director postings from companies like OpenAI, Anthropic, Datadog, and GitLab. What gets someone hired at the mid level, based on the titles and skill tags we see, is direct experience securing at least one major cloud platform in production, not just coursework or certifications. At senior and staff level, postings add scope, such as leading incident response, owning security architecture across multiple cloud accounts, or managing a team, as seen in the CoreWeave and Nebius manager level roles in our data. Very few postings in our sample look like a first job in security, so this discipline reads as a track someone moves into after already building general security or infrastructure experience.\n\nAWS, Azure, and GCP are the three cloud platforms named repeatedly across our sample, often together in the same posting when the employer runs a multi cloud environment. Kubernetes, Terraform, Docker, and CI/CD tooling show up constantly alongside them, reflecting how much of this work now happens through infrastructure as code and containers rather than manually configuring servers. Python shows up as the most common scripting language in the skills data, with Rust, Go, and Bash also present in a smaller number of postings. Security specific tools and concepts named directly in our data include IAM, SIEM, EDR, zero trust, threat modeling, incident response, vulnerability management, and cryptography, which together describe the actual toolkit rather than a wish list. A smaller cluster of postings, mostly at AI labs like OpenAI and Anthropic, also asks for lower level skills like C++ and reverse engineering tied to hardware and platform security work.\n\nAs of today we currently track approximately 52 open postings tagged Cloud and Infrastructure Security in our live database, out of a broader working sample of 104 postings we pulled for this guide. Of those 104, 45 postings carried an actual stated salary figure, and among just those 45 the range ran from about $78,581 to $202,300 a year, with most falling between roughly $125,000 and $185,000. That range includes both mid level and senior roles, so it should be read as a spread across the whole discipline rather than a single number for one level. The companies appearing most often in our sample include OpenAI, Datadog, Anthropic, SpaceXAI, Booz Allen Hamilton, and Vanguard, each showing up across multiple open roles rather than a single posting. Because our sample mixes AI labs, defense contractors, and traditional enterprises, the specific mix of skills and frameworks an employer asks for varies quite a bit by sector even within this one discipline.",
